{"name":"Aqualyx","deep":true,"image":null,"mentions":54,"casual":{"lead":["Aqualyx is a CE-marked injectable made from deoxycholic acid, a bile acid that dissolves fat cell membranes. In Europe it is sold to trained practitioners for reducing small pockets of fat such as under the chin.","In looksmaxxing forums it is discussed as a way to slim the cheeks, jaw and neck area. Published clinical evidence for these uses is limited to small studies and case reports, and no published trial has tested facial-hollowing as described in forums."],"sections":[{"h":"How it works","paras":["Deoxycholate acts as a detergent on fat cell membranes, causing cells to break open; the body then clears the debris, with inflammation and later fibrosis described in the treated area. The related drug Kybella is the US-approved deoxycholic acid product for submental fat."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks and caveats","paras":["Reported effects are swelling, bruising and pain. A published case described chronic infection and nodule formation after a deoxycholate injection. The product is not FDA-approved, counterfeits have been discussed in forums, and the manufacturer sells only to trained professionals; self-injection is outside labelled use."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[],"links":{"studies":[{"title":"Lipoma removal using a high-frequency ultrasound-guided injection of a Class III CE-marked device-Empirical findings.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30027646/","source":"Journal of cosmetic dermatology","year":2019,"cites":3,"kind":null},{"title":"Chronic Infection and Nodule Formation following Deoxycholate Injection.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35832164/","source":"Archives of plastic surgery","year":2022,"cites":2,"kind":null},{"title":"The efficacy of high-frequency ultrasound-guided injection lipolysis in reducing fat deposits located on the inside of the knees.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33500794/","source":"Journal of ultrasonography","year":2021,"cites":null,"k